\description{
\code{fit_etas_pp()} alternates between maximum-likelihood estimation of the ETAS
triggering component and, when requested, a weighted Poisson fit for the
background component. The posterior probability that each event is a
background event is used as its fractional weight in the next background
update.
}
\details{
Fits a spatial or temporal epidemic-type aftershock sequence model using the
triggering parametrization implemented in \code{etasFLP}, while estimating the
spatial background through the weighted Poisson point-process machinery of
\code{ppnexus}.

For an event history \eqn{\mathcal{H}_t}, the fitted conditional intensity
has the decomposition

\deqn{\lambda(t, \mathbf{s} \mid \mathcal{H}_t)
= \mu b(\mathbf{s};\boldsymbol{\beta})
+ \sum_{t_j<t} h(t-t_j, \mathbf{s}-\mathbf{s}_j,
m_j;\boldsymbol{\theta}),}

where \eqn{b(\mathbf{s};\boldsymbol{\beta})} is a normalized spatial
background density and \eqn{h()} is the ETAS offspring contribution. The
seven core ETAS parameters are \code{mu}, \code{k0}, \code{c}, \code{p}, \code{gamma}, \code{d}, and \code{q}.
In the spatial model, \code{c} and \code{p} control temporal decay, \code{d} and \code{q} control
spatial decay, \code{gamma} controls magnitude-dependent spatial scaling, and
\code{k0} controls offspring productivity. Additional productivity effects enter
through the linear predictor defined by \code{formula1}, \code{betacov}, and \code{offset}.

The log likelihood is the standard conditional point-process likelihood,

\deqn{\ell(\boldsymbol{\theta})
= \sum_i \log\{\lambda(t_i,\mathbf{s}_i)\}
- \int_T \int_W \lambda(t,\mathbf{s})\,d\mathbf{s}\,dt.}

Triggering contributions are evaluated by the registered Fortran routine in
\code{etasFLP}. Spatial triggering integrals are numerically approximated over the
rectangular working region using \code{ntheta} angular directions.

Unless \code{is.backconstant = TRUE}, the background model is fitted with
\code{\link[=fit_pp]{fit_pp()}} on the two-dimensional working coordinates \code{x} and \code{y}. Its fitted
ground intensity is normalized to integrate to one over the spatial window;
the ETAS parameter \code{mu} then scales the background rate. During declustering,
the eventwise posterior probabilities

\deqn{\rho_i = \frac{\mu b(\mathbf{s}_i)}
{\lambda(t_i,\mathbf{s}_i)}}

are used as fractional event weights in the next Poisson background fit.
Iteration stops when both the maximum change in posterior background
probabilities and the maximum relative parameter change fall below their
respective tolerances, or when \code{ndeclust} iterations have been completed.

\code{onlytime = TRUE} fits the temporal special case: the background is constant,
declustering is disabled, and \code{gamma}, \code{d}, and \code{q} are fixed to zero.
\code{is.backconstant = TRUE} similarly disables alternating background updates
and uses a uniform spatial density.

Several arguments are retained to ease comparison with legacy
\code{etasFLP::etasclass} interfaces. At present, \code{time.update = TRUE},
\code{flp = TRUE}, and non-\code{NULL} \code{cat.back} generate errors;
\code{thinning = TRUE} generates a warning and is ignored. The arguments
\code{magn.threshold.back}, \code{hdef}, \code{hvarx}, \code{hvary}, \code{m1}, \code{back.smooth},
\code{compsqm}, and \code{epsmax} are validated or stored for compatibility but do not
alter the current parametric-background estimation algorithm.
}
\examples{
set.seed(123)
n <- 35
w <- pp_window(
  lower = c(x = 0, y = 0, time = 0),
  upper = c(x = 1, y = 1, time = 100),
  axis_roles = c(x = "space", y = "space", time = "time")
)
pattern <- pp_pattern(
  data = data.frame(
    x = runif(n),
    y = runif(n),
    time = sort(runif(n, 0, 100)),
    z = runif(n, 1, 20),
    magn1 = 2.5 + rexp(n, 2)
  ),
  coords = c("x", "y", "time"),
  marks = c("z", "magn1"),
  window = w
)
catalog <- etas_catalog(pattern, longlat.to.km = FALSE)

\donttest{
fit <- fit_etas_pp(
  catalog,
  magn.threshold = 2.5,
  formula1 = "time ~ magnitude - 1",
  formula.bg = ~ x + y,
  ndeclust = 2,
  min.declust.iter = 1,
  iterlim = 20,
  verbose.convergence = FALSE,
  verbose = FALSE,
  trace = FALSE
)
fit
summary(fit)
}

}
